  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nA weak front pushing south through the region will usher in drier \nnortheasterly flow today. Drier air aloft will mix down with dew \npoints falling into the 30s across northern and eastern areas of the \ncwa this afternoon. RAP/HRRR are typically good at handling these \nsituations and both indicate RH's falling into the 15-20% range for \nareas along and north of Route 13 in southern IL, southwest IN, and \nthe Pennyrile of west KY. Thankfully winds will be light, generally \n5 mph at most. Despite the light winds, the RH's are low enough to \nlead to elevated fire weather conditions, so have issued an SPS for \nthis. \n\nWinds become southeasterly on Friday, which will lead to a very warm \nday ahead of the tropical system set to impact our region this \nweekend. Latest guidance is hinting at some 90 degree readings being \npossible, which may threaten daily record highs. Dew points creep \nupwards across the western 2/3rds of the region, but may remain \nquite low across the KY Pennyrile and southwest IN. This may lead to \nanother day of RH's falling below 20% in these areas, as depicted by \nthe HRRR/RAP. Winds will also be a bit stronger on Friday, generally \nbetween 5 and 10 mph. So another day of elevated fire weather is \npossible, mainly across our east.\n\nHurricane Isaias has formed in the southern Gulf and will move \nnortheastward over the next 48 hours with a forecast landfall \nsomewhere near Mobile/Pensacola Friday evening/night. The track has \nshifted east compared to 24 hours ago and also slightly faster. As \nthe remnants get ingested into a shortwave trough sweeping through \nthe lower Mississippi/Tennessee Valleys, moisture and large scale \nlift will quickly move north into our area on Saturday. A large \nshield of moderate to heavy rain will likely reach our southern \nborder by late morning/midday and pivot north/northwestward through \nthe afternoon. The heaviest rains should gradually wind down \novernight Saturday night into Sunday morning, leaving behind mostly \ndry conditions by Sunday afternoon (except possibly our far east).\n\nQPF amounts haven't changed much, still generally thinking a good 1 \nto 1.5\". Although will likely be less than that across western \nportions of the region from the Ozark Foothills northeast into \nportions of southwest Illinois (closer to Mt Vernon). Meanwhile, \nlocalized higher amounts pushing 2\" or slightly higher remain \npossible, particularly across west KY. If the system trends stronger \ninto a major hurricane at landfall, then it's possible rainfall \namounts trend higher and overachieve again which would lend to a \ngreater flooding concern.\n\n850-925mb winds continue to look quite strong as the remnants of \nIsaias push north into the TN Valley on Saturday. However, a stable \nlayer near the surface may keep the strongest winds from mixing down \nas it approaches our cwa. For now, generally think wind gusts of 25-\n35 mph (highest near KY/TN border), are the most probable outcome \nSaturday afternoon and evening. Certainly still possible for wind \nadvisory level gusts of 40+ mph, probably would put that around 20-\n30% chance of happening.\n\nUpper level ridging builds back in early next week, which will allow \nhighs to return to the 80s Monday and possibly Tuesday. A backdoor \nfront (likely dry) drops through on Tuesday though which will allow \ncooler air to filter in mid week.",
